483 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 127 0 R/T(In Greek Myths, Prophecy Is a Mirror, Not a Warning In Greek thought, the Moirae, or the Fates, were above all. Even Zeus, king of the gods, feared them. Their threads wove the destinies of mortals and immortals alike, and once spun, no blade or bolt could sever them.)/E(In Greek Myths, Prophecy Is a Mirror, Not a Warning In Greek thought, the Moirae, or the Fates, were above all. Even Zeus, king of the gods, feared them. Their threads wove the destinies of mortals and immortals alike, and once spun, no blade or bolt could sever them.)/Pg 27 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

484 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 127 0 R/T(When the gods tried to change destiny, they didn\220t just fail\204they often made things worse. The more they interfered, the more human they seemed, caught in cycles of pride, fear, and stubbornness. The Greeks didn\220t depict their gods as flawless beings; instead, their gods were flawed, emotional, and often afraid. Nothing scared them more than a future they couldn\220t control.)/E(When the gods tried to change destiny, they didn\220t just fail\204they often made things worse. The more they interfered, the more human they seemed, caught in cycles of pride, fear, and stubbornness. The Greeks didn\220t depict their gods as flawless beings; instead, their gods were flawed, emotional, and often afraid. Nothing scared them more than a future they couldn\220t control.)/Pg 27 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

485 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 127 0 R/T(The Greeks, who built their temples in marble and told their stories with drama, understood something timeless: the more we try to control the future, the more we become trapped by our response to it.)/E(The Greeks, who built their temples in marble and told their stories with drama, understood something timeless: the more we try to control the future, the more we become trapped by our response to it.)/Pg 27 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

486 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 127 0 R/T(The One Who Utters Fate\220s Inevitable Truths In Greek myth, prophecies were often like legal contracts; you couldn\220t break them, only misread them. You might think the Greek gods would have learned to stop consulting the Oracle of Delphi, the priestess who spoke for Apollo and delivered famously cryptic prophecies. Yet mortals and gods alike kept coming back, desperate to bend fate to their will.)/E(The One Who Utters Fate\220s Inevitable Truths In Greek myth, prophecies were often like legal contracts; you couldn\220t break them, only misread them. You might think the Greek gods would have learned to stop consulting the Oracle of Delphi, the priestess who spoke for Apollo and delivered famously cryptic prophecies. Yet mortals and gods alike kept coming back, desperate to bend fate to their will.)/Pg 27 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

487 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 128 0 R/T(But the oracle wasn\220t a fortune cookie. Her words were layered with irony, as Croesus, king of Lydia, discovered. Told he would "destroy a great empire" if he attacked Persia, he went to war and ended up destroying his own.)/E(But the oracle wasn\220t a fortune cookie. Her words were layered with irony, as Croesus, king of Lydia, discovered. Told he would "destroy a great empire" if he attacked Persia, he went to war and ended up destroying his own.)/Pg 28 0 R/K[0]>>
