501 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 130 0 R/T(Oedipus: The Man Who Walked Into Fate Oedipus was not born a monster. He was a man who wanted to be better than the fate handed to him. When a prophecy declared that he would kill his father and marry his mother, he ran away, not out of fear, but to protect those he believed were his parents.)/E(Oedipus: The Man Who Walked Into Fate Oedipus was not born a monster. He was a man who wanted to be better than the fate handed to him. When a prophecy declared that he would kill his father and marry his mother, he ran away, not out of fear, but to protect those he believed were his parents.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

502 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 130 0 R/T(But in Greek myth, fate is a cruel companion. The very road he chose to escape disaster became the one that plunged him into it. He solved riddles no man could and ruled a kingdom with wisdom, only to fall apart when the truth came limping out. When the prophecy fulfilled itself, Oedipus did not curse the gods. Instead, he took all the pain upon himself. He gouged out his eyes with his own hands, preferring blindness to the torturous sight of a painful truth. In his suffering, he became more than a myth. He became a mirror for every person who has ever had to ask, Am I the villain in my own story)/E(But in Greek myth, fate is a cruel companion. The very road he chose to escape disaster became the one that plunged him into it. He solved riddles no man could and ruled a kingdom with wisdom, only to fall apart when the truth came limping out. When the prophecy fulfilled itself, Oedipus did not curse the gods. Instead, he took all the pain upon himself. He gouged out his eyes with his own hands, preferring blindness to the torturous sight of a painful truth. In his suffering, he became more than a myth. He became a mirror for every person who has ever had to ask, Am I the villain in my own story)/Pg 30 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

503 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 130 0 R/T(Achilles: The Warrior Who Chose Glory Over Life He was no ordinary warrior; he was a bright flame: brilliant, hot, and soon extinguished. Born of a goddess yet loved by mortals, he was offered a choice: die old and forgotten, or die young and be remembered forever. He chose the latter.)/E(Achilles: The Warrior Who Chose Glory Over Life He was no ordinary warrior; he was a bright flame: brilliant, hot, and soon extinguished. Born of a goddess yet loved by mortals, he was offered a choice: die old and forgotten, or die young and be remembered forever. He chose the latter.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

504 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 130 0 R/T(Yet behind the armor and rage was a deeply loving man. His wrath in The Iliad was not born of pride alone, but of profound grief for Patroclus, his closest friend, his heart. Achilles\220 tragedy was that the cost of eternal remembrance was everything. He gained songs, statues, and centuries of retelling, yes\204but at the price of a short life haunted by a choice he could never escape. His is a story of glory burned at the altar of immortality, a painful reminder that glory is cold comfort for the dead.)/E(Yet behind the armor and rage was a deeply loving man. His wrath in The Iliad was not born of pride alone, but of profound grief for Patroclus, his closest friend, his heart. Achilles\220 tragedy was that the cost of eternal remembrance was everything. He gained songs, statues, and centuries of retelling, yes\204but at the price of a short life haunted by a choice he could never escape. His is a story of glory burned at the altar of immortality, a painful reminder that glory is cold comfort for the dead.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

