<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(Where Legends End and Humanity Begins It is the pain, not the power, that binds these heroes to us.)/E(Where Legends End and Humanity Begins It is the pain, not the power, that binds these heroes to us.)/Pg 31 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

514 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(Oedipus sought truth and paid for it with his world. Achilles chose immortality and forfeited peace. Antigone stood for justice and was buried by it. Orpheus gave everything for love and lost it in a heartbeat.)/E(Oedipus sought truth and paid for it with his world. Achilles chose immortality and forfeited peace. Antigone stood for justice and was buried by it. Orpheus gave everything for love and lost it in a heartbeat.)/Pg 31 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

515 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(These stories live on because they are us, magnified. They stumble, they rage, they hope too hard, and they love too much. In that way, they earned their immortality not in stone or ash, but in story, because the most unforgettable heroes are those who dared to feel.)/E(These stories live on because they are us, magnified. They stumble, they rage, they hope too hard, and they love too much. In that way, they earned their immortality not in stone or ash, but in story, because the most unforgettable heroes are those who dared to feel.)/Pg 31 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

516 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(They remind us that even the brightest light casts a long shadow, that strength doesn't always roar, and that greatness is not always gentle. They were flawed, but never lesser. Doomed, but not defeated. They died, yes, but in a way that makes us ache for them still.)/E(They remind us that even the brightest light casts a long shadow, that strength doesn't always roar, and that greatness is not always gentle. They were flawed, but never lesser. Doomed, but not defeated. They died, yes, but in a way that makes us ache for them still.)/Pg 31 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

517 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(And maybe that\220s the heart of tragedy: not the fall, but the fact that we still wish they had risen. That despite the myths and the distance, we see ourselves in their fire, their choices, and their heartbreak.)/E(And maybe that\220s the heart of tragedy: not the fall, but the fact that we still wish they had risen. That despite the myths and the distance, we see ourselves in their fire, their choices, and their heartbreak.)/Pg 31 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

518 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(In the end, the gods may rule Olympus,)/E(In the end, the gods may rule Olympus,)/Pg 31 0 R/K[10]>>
