519 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 131 0 R/T(But it\220s the mortals who rule memory.)/E(But it\220s the mortals who rule memory.)/Pg 31 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

520 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(We all know the names: Medusa, Helen, Pandora. They\220ve been part of our culture forever. But most of us only know the version of their stories that has been passed down for centuries: the villain, the troublemaker, the one who ruined everything. What we don\220t always hear is the full picture. This piece looks at how some of the most famous women in Greek mythology were misunderstood, rewritten, or blamed\204and how modern voices are finally telling their stories differently.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

521 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(Medusa: From Monster to Protector)/Pg 32 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

522 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(Let\220s start with Medusa, because her story is the ultimate glow-up. You\220ve heard the basics: snakes for hair, a stone-cold stare, and hero fodder for Perseus. But here\220s the part they don\220t always cover in mythology class. Before the snakes, Medusa was a priestess in Athena\220s temple. Poseidon, the sea god, assaulted her there. Athena\220s response? Blame Medusa, turn her into a gorgon, and let heroes like Perseus hunt her head as a trophy. For centuries, that was her legacy\204a warning to women about divine punishment.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

523 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(But today? Medusa\220s story is being rewritten. Artists like Luciano Garbati flipped her narrative with his sculpture Medusa With the Head of Perseus, where she stands calm and victorious, holding Perseus\220s severed head. Feminists reclaim her snakes as armor, her gaze as a boundary. Instead of a monster, she\220s a survivor. TikTok trends even joke: \215Medusa didn\220t turn men to stone\204she just stopped giving them the time of day.\216 Her myth isn\220t about fear anymore. It\220s about agency.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

524 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(Helen of Troy: More Than a Pretty Face)/Pg 32 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

525 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(Helen\220s reputation is stuck on one line: \215the face that launched a thousand ships.\216 But let\220s unpack that. Was she stolen by Paris, or did she choose to leave her husband, Menelaus? The myths waffle. In Euripides\220 play Helen, she never even went to Troy\204Zeus replaced her with a ghostly double. The real Helen spent the war waiting in Egypt, her voice erased by poets who needed a scapegoat.)/Pg 32 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

526 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 132 0 R/T(FROM VICTIMS TO VOICES: RECLAIMING THE WOMEN OF GREEK MYTHOLOGY)/E(FROM VICTIMS TO VOICES: RECLAIMING THE WOMEN OF GREEK MYTHOLOGY)/Pg 32 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

527 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(Insaaf Imthiyas)/Pg 32 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

528 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 132 0 R/E(III Year, B. Tech. CSE)/Pg 32 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

529 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(Modern retellings fix this. Novelists like Margaret Atwood in The Penelopiad and historian Bettany Hughes in Helen of Troy: Goddess, Princess, Whore give her complexity. She\220s not just a runaway wife; she\220s a woman navigating a world where gods and men pull the strings. The lesson? Helen\220s story isn\220t just about beauty\204it\220s about how history erases women\220s choices.)/E(Modern retellings fix this. Novelists like Margaret Atwood in The Penelopiad and historian Bettany Hughes in Helen of Troy: Goddess, Princess, Whore give her complexity. She\220s not just a runaway wife; she\220s a woman navigating a world where gods and men pull the strings. The lesson? Helen\220s story isn\220t just about beauty\204it\220s about how history erases women\220s choices.)/Pg 33 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

530 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(Clytemnestra: The Mother Who Roared Clytemnestra is often reduced to \215the queen who murdered her husband.\216 But here\220s the rest: her husband, Agamemnon, sacrificed their daughter Iphigenia to get good winds for his ships to Troy. He slit her throat on an altar while Clytemnestra watched. Years later, when Agamemnon returns victorious, Clytemnestra stabs him in the bath. The ancient poets called her a monster. Aeschylus\220s play Agamemnon paints her as a \215woman with a man\220s heart.\216)/E(Clytemnestra: The Mother Who Roared Clytemnestra is often reduced to \215the queen who murdered her husband.\216 But here\220s the rest: her husband, Agamemnon, sacrificed their daughter Iphigenia to get good winds for his ships to Troy. He slit her throat on an altar while Clytemnestra watched. Years later, when Agamemnon returns victorious, Clytemnestra stabs him in the bath. The ancient poets called her a monster. Aeschylus\220s play Agamemnon paints her as a \215woman with a man\220s heart.\216)/Pg 33 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

531 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(But modern writers like Pat Barker in The Silence of the Girls flip the script. In her version, Clytemnestra isn\220t a villain\204she\220s a mother shattered by grief. Her rage isn\220t madness; it\220s love turned inside out. The real tragedy isn\220t her revenge\204it\220s that she had to choose between silence and violence.)/E(But modern writers like Pat Barker in The Silence of the Girls flip the script. In her version, Clytemnestra isn\220t a villain\204she\220s a mother shattered by grief. Her rage isn\220t madness; it\220s love turned inside out. The real tragedy isn\220t her revenge\204it\220s that she had to choose between silence and violence.)/Pg 33 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

532 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(Pandora: The First Woman & the Jar of Hope Pandora gets the worst PR in mythology. Zeus created her as a \215punishment\216 for Prometheus stealing fire. He gave her a jar \(not a box, blame mistranslations!\) filled with misery and sent her to Earth. She opens it, unleashing suffering, and gets blamed for eternity. But hold on\204the jar also held hope. Why does no one talk about that?)/E(Pandora: The First Woman & the Jar of Hope Pandora gets the worst PR in mythology. Zeus created her as a \215punishment\216 for Prometheus stealing fire. He gave her a jar \(not a box, blame mistranslations!\) filled with misery and sent her to Earth. She opens it, unleashing suffering, and gets blamed for eternity. But hold on\204the jar also held hope. Why does no one talk about that?)/Pg 33 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

533 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(Because hope is dangerous. Hope means people might question the gods, rebel, or rewrite their fate. Natalie Haynes\220s book Pandora\220s Jar reframes her: What if Pandora wasn\220t naive, but curious? What if opening the jar wasn\220t a mistake, but an act of bravery? Today, she\220s a symbol of resilience\204not a scapegoat, but the first woman daring enough to seek answers.)/E(Because hope is dangerous. Hope means people might question the gods, rebel, or rewrite their fate. Natalie Haynes\220s book Pandora\220s Jar reframes her: What if Pandora wasn\220t naive, but curious? What if opening the jar wasn\220t a mistake, but an act of bravery? Today, she\220s a symbol of resilience\204not a scapegoat, but the first woman daring enough to seek answers.)/Pg 33 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

534 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 133 0 R/T(Circe: The Witch Who Found Her Island In Homer\220s Odyssey, Circe is the witch who turns men into pigs. Odysseus outsmarts her, beds her, and leaves. Classic hero stuff. But Madeline Miller's novel Circe gives her a voice. Exiled on an island for her power, Circe isn\220t a monster\204she\220s a woman learning to survive. She masters witchcraft not to harm, but to protect herself. She falls in love, raises a son, and chooses solitude over subservience. Miller\220s Circe isn\220t defined by the men in her life. She\220s defined by her choices. The message? Power isn\220t about turning men into pigs\204it\220s about refusing to be turned into one yourself.)/E(Circe: The Witch Who Found Her Island In Homer\220s Odyssey, Circe is the witch who turns men into pigs. Odysseus outsmarts her, beds her, and leaves. Classic hero stuff. But Madeline Miller's novel Circe gives her a voice. Exiled on an island for her power, Circe isn\220t a monster\204she\220s a woman learning to survive. She masters witchcraft not to harm, but to protect herself. She falls in love, raises a son, and chooses solitude over subservience. Miller\220s Circe isn\220t defined by the men in her life. She\220s defined by her choices. The message? Power isn\220t about turning men into pigs\204it\220s about refusing to be turned into one yourself.)/Pg 33 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

535 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 134 0 R/T(Persephone: Queen of the Underworld Persephone\220s myth is usually told as a kidnapping: Hades drags her to the underworld, her mother Demeter, mourns, and the seasons change. But modern poets like Aimee Nezhukumatathil, in \215On Listening to Your Teacher Lecture About Persephone,\216 reimagine her as a queen. Her pomegranate seeds aren\220t a trap\204they\220re a choice. She rules the underworld not as a prisoner, but as its sovereign. This shift matters. Persephone isn\220t a victim of \215bad boys\216 or overprotective moms. She\220s a woman claiming her throne, her story a reminder that darkness can be a place of power.)/E(Persephone: Queen of the Underworld Persephone\220s myth is usually told as a kidnapping: Hades drags her to the underworld, her mother Demeter, mourns, and the seasons change. But modern poets like Aimee Nezhukumatathil, in \215On Listening to Your Teacher Lecture About Persephone,\216 reimagine her as a queen. Her pomegranate seeds aren\220t a trap\204they\220re a choice. She rules the underworld not as a prisoner, but as its sovereign. This shift matters. Persephone isn\220t a victim of \215bad boys\216 or overprotective moms. She\220s a woman claiming her throne, her story a reminder that darkness can be a place of power.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

536 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 134 0 R/T(The Furies: From Monsters to Justice Keepers The Furies\204vengeance incarnate, with bat wings and bloodshot eyes\204terrorized oath-breakers in ancient myths. In Aeschylus\220s The Eumenides, Athena convinces them to become the \215kindly ones,\216 trading vengeance for justice. But feminist readings spot the subtext: their primal fury was neutered to fit a patriarchal society. Playwright Anne Carson undoes this in her translation of The Bakkhai. Her Furies aren\220t tamed; they\220re raw, unapologetic, a force of nature. The lesson? Women\220s rage isn\220t a flaw\204it\220s a language.)/E(The Furies: From Monsters to Justice Keepers The Furies\204vengeance incarnate, with bat wings and bloodshot eyes\204terrorized oath-breakers in ancient myths. In Aeschylus\220s The Eumenides, Athena convinces them to become the \215kindly ones,\216 trading vengeance for justice. But feminist readings spot the subtext: their primal fury was neutered to fit a patriarchal society. Playwright Anne Carson undoes this in her translation of The Bakkhai. Her Furies aren\220t tamed; they\220re raw, unapologetic, a force of nature. The lesson? Women\220s rage isn\220t a flaw\204it\220s a language.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

537 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 134 0 R/T(Arachne: The Weaver Who Challenged a Goddess Arachne, a mortal weaver, dared to say she was better than Athena. They held a contest where Arachne\220s tapestry mocked the gods\220 cruelty. Athena, enraged, turned her into a spider. The traditional moral was \215Don\220t challenge the gods.\216 But today, Arachne is a symbol of creative defiance. Her story isn\220t about hubris\204it\220s about the cost of silencing inconvenient truths. Writers like Jennifer Saint see her as a pioneer: the first artist punished for telling the truth.)/E(Arachne: The Weaver Who Challenged a Goddess Arachne, a mortal weaver, dared to say she was better than Athena. They held a contest where Arachne\220s tapestry mocked the gods\220 cruelty. Athena, enraged, turned her into a spider. The traditional moral was \215Don\220t challenge the gods.\216 But today, Arachne is a symbol of creative defiance. Her story isn\220t about hubris\204it\220s about the cost of silencing inconvenient truths. Writers like Jennifer Saint see her as a pioneer: the first artist punished for telling the truth.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

538 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 134 0 R/T(Why These Stories Matter Today These myths aren\220t ancient history\204they\220re blueprints that show how stories shape power. For centuries, women were cast as villains or victims to uphold patriarchal norms. But retellings do more than \215fix\216 myths; they reclaim the right to be complicated.)/E(Why These Stories Matter Today These myths aren\220t ancient history\204they\220re blueprints that show how stories shape power. For centuries, women were cast as villains or victims to uphold patriarchal norms. But retellings do more than \215fix\216 myths; they reclaim the right to be complicated.)/Pg 34 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

