<</Title(CROSSWORD)/Dest[24 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2232 0 R/Next 2237 0 R>>
endobj

2236 0 obj
<</Title(The Furies: From Monsters to Justice Keepers The Furies\204vengeance incarnate, with bat wings and bloodshot eyes\204terrorized oath-breakers in ancient myths. In Aeschylus\220s The Eumenides, Athena convinces them to become the \215kindly ones,\216 trading vengeance for justice. But feminist readings spot the subtext: their primal fury was neutered to fit a patriarchal society. Playwright Anne Carson undoes this in her translation of The Bakkhai. Her Furies aren\220t tamed; they\220re raw, unapologetic, a force of nature. The lesson? Women\220s rage isn\220t a flaw\204it\220s a language.)/Dest[34 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2238 0 R/Next 2233 0 R>>
endobj

2237 0 obj
<</Title(ACROSS)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2235 0 R/Next 2239 0 R>>
endobj

2238 0 obj
<</Title(Persephone: Queen of the Underworld Persephone\220s myth is usually told as a kidnapping: Hades drags her to the underworld, her mother Demeter, mourns, and the seasons change. But modern poets like Aimee Nezhukumatathil, in \215On Listening to Your Teacher Lecture About Persephone,\216 reimagine her as a queen. Her pomegranate seeds aren\220t a trap\204they\220re a choice. She rules the underworld not as a prisoner, but as its sovereign. This shift matters. Persephone isn\220t a victim of \215bad boys\216 or overprotective moms. She\220s a woman claiming her throne, her story a reminder that darkness can be a place of power.)/Dest[34 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2240 0 R/Next 2236 0 R>>
endobj

2239 0 obj
<</Title(DOWN)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2237 0 R/First 2241 0 R/Last 2241 0 R/Count -1/Next 2242 0 R>>
endobj

2240 0 obj
<</Title(Circe: The Witch Who Found Her Island In Homer\220s Odyssey, Circe is the witch who turns men into pigs. Odysseus outsmarts her, beds her, and leaves. Classic hero stuff. But Madeline Miller's novel Circe gives her a voice. Exiled on an island for her power, Circe isn\220t a monster\204she\220s a woman learning to survive. She masters witchcraft not to harm, but to protect herself. She falls in love, raises a son, and chooses solitude over subservience. Miller\220s Circe isn\220t defined by the men in her life. She\220s defined by her choices. The message? Power isn\220t about turning men into pigs\204it\220s about refusing to be turned into one yourself.)/Dest[33 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 2243 0 R/Next 2238 0 R>>
endobj

2241 0 obj
<</Title(IDEATED BY  LIONAL MAHENDRA RAJA III YEAR, B. TECH. AI & DS)/Dest[25 0 R/Fit]/Parent 2239 0 R>>
